<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">835–882</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Louis III the Younger · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis the Younger was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> from 876 to 882, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>. He ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and in 876 defeated his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> when Charles tried to seize the western lands of his inheritance. He married Liutgard, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Liudolf, and was buried, like his father, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> Abbey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger.jpg" alt="Louis the Younger" width="194" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekkehard von Aura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_the_Younger_of_Saxony.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis the Younger, born between 830 and 835 and sometimes called Louis the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> or Louis III, was the second of three sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and Queen Hemma, after Carloman and before Charles. In 854, at the invitation of Aquitainian nobles opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> and Pepin II, he led an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> to receive the Aquitainian crown and marched as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> before turning back.</p><p>He formed close ties with the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, and in 865 he and his brother Charles rebelled against their father. They were reconciled that year, and the elder Louis divided the remaining territories between them: Louis received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. In 869 he married Liutgard, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liudolf-duke-of-saxony">Liudolf, Duke of Saxony</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>. She was politically ambitious and spurred him to pursue ambitious goals. The match increased tensions with his father, and he rebelled again in 871 and 873, reconciling each time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> on 28 August 876, the three brothers succeeded under the earlier partition, Carloman taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Pannonian March, Louis Franconia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and Charles Alamannia. Louis buried his father at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a>, in his own territory, to stress his primacy, and kept his chief adviser, Liutbert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. On 8 October 876 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> he defeated a much larger host of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, who had tried to annex the eastern parts of Lotharingia.</p><p>In 878 Louis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> promised each other to respect the succession of their sons by the Treaty of Fouron. When the Stammerer died in April 879, western nobles invited Louis the Younger to rule the western kingdom. He invaded as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and retreated after the new kings ceded their part of Lotharingia, which the Treaty of Ribemont confirmed in February 880. Carloman, struck by a stroke in 879, named Louis his successor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which he held outright from 880.</p><p>From 879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> raided deeply into the Frankish lands. In February 880 Louis defeated a Norse host at the Battle of Thimeon, near modern Charleroi, in which his illegitimate son Hugh was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis fell sick in 881 and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 20 January 882, and was buried beside his father at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a>. With no heir, his territories passed to his brother Charles, who reunited the whole East Frankish kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis the Younger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Count Liudolf, who died in 866, held lands in eastern Saxony and is described in some sources as dux; with his wife Oda he founded the convent of Gandersheim in 852, which became the family’s religious centre, and his daughter Liutgard married the Carolingian king <b>Louis the Younger</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Lüneburg Heath, in today's Lower Saxony, the Norse Great Heathen Army defeated the army of King <b>Louis the Younger</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Andernach, the East Frankish king <b>Louis the Younger</b> defeated his uncle, the West Frankish king and emperor Charles the Bald, who had tried to seize the western lands of Louis's inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The East Frankish king Louis the German and his son <b>Louis the Younger</b> were buried there.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-lorsch">Imperial Abbey of Lorsch</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.11,8.68&amp;year=865" aria-label="Frankfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4749 421 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Louis the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5199 691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.11,8.68&amp;year=865">What was here in 865 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
